t0 History

t1 Compilers in General

t2 Creating a programming language

p Creating a language is not a small thing, especially when it is \
multi-purpose and with a high range of abstraction. The internet is \
full of people saying this is bad to create its new language if it \
produces the same result as the good old ones at the end.

p However, among them, how many really knew what it cost to make a \
compiler ? Stop being paralyzed about those who talk, step aside \
those who code.



t2 Why you should create your programming language

p Let us be clear one second. \
Creating a compiler, no matter how hard it is, is the best way to \
develop skills in terms of algorithmic strategies, vast project \
management, step by step problem solving and so on...

p However, before engaging in such a project, have in mind that \
compilation principle, in a low-level extend, requires \bway\b more \
in \uyour head\u than in the code itself. Before being even able to \
have an idea of what you are going to make, you first need to acquire \
all the information about the result you want to produce.

p Here already, getting that knowledge can be a real nightmare if you \
don't know where to look for, the documentations you have talks so \
much about useless stuff but the essential is missing... If you go \
deep enough, you may even find partially correct documents, last \
updated years ago by some random guy, posted online by someone else \
who still had it on his home cloud... or worst, inexistant documents.

p Anyway, consider that, depending on what you are going to do, the \
first thing to do is to know where you go, so that you can draw the \
line between the input you want to have and the result you want to \
produce out of it. Seems pretty obvious isn't it ?

l \u\bNow listen to this carefully:\b\u

p Why do I insist that much on getting knowledge ? \
Is it not the compiler code in itself the hardest thing to do ?

p Indeed, after having made ZINC, I can tell you now that the longest \
part was to code the compiler in itself, you're right. \
However, \uas soon as you know the boundaries of your project, you \
should be able to measure how hard it will be to make it.\u

p By consequent, you can decide \uright now\u, \cff0000\bbefore \
having written one line of code\b\c, if you really want to invest \
yourself in \it or not.

p These warnings you will find on the internet saying "making your \
own compiler is bad" are not completely to reject. They mean "don't \
run too fast, you probably don't know how hard and how loooooooooong \
it is to achieve !".



t2 Why you should NOT create your programming language

p I know, people wants to code. But people are willing to make funny \
things when they code, or powerful projects by using their low-level \
layers of applications. However, making a compiler is not that much \
funny. Why ?

p Well, if I asked you to write down every single mechanism involved \
in a C to ASM compiler for example, you would certainly require a \
gigantic university whiteboard to draw them all, and I am not sure it \
would be enough though.

p Now, considering you have the whole plan for your compiler in head \
and you write it down. Congrats! You made yourself a working C to ASM \
compiler! Wonderful! Now, if you make some mistakes, you have error \
messages, cool, but if it works, \uit seems like\u you have the same \
result as if you were using any other C compiler.

p Even if you know what changes, or anything, everything you can \
really be proud of, and enjoy about your project, everything is \
either in your head, in some hidden lines of code in your compiler \
project, or in an unreadable-for-human binary format file.

l \uNo, there is not that much "fun" in making a compiler.\u


t2 Available open source compilers

p Making its own compiler can be a really good experience for someone \
really passionate, eager to know more, even if the goal is not to \
conquire the entire planet with it. There are a lot of open source \
compilers online, claiming to be made for "academic purpose" or even \
sometimes "for fun".

p Looking at the size and complexity of these projects, often taken \
over by dozens of people over the years, improving the thing again \
and again: for most of them, \uthey are no longer just tiny \
projects\u made "for fun".

p You don't have to be ashame to propose your compiler to the world. \
No one asked you to make the best compiler ever, especially if you \
worked on it alone and learned everything by yourself, on your free \
time.

p We don't all have contacts all over the world to guide us on \
the right places to look for information, or have been in computing \
since childhood.

p The world of low-level development has always been a subject of \
repulsion, most of the time criticsized by people that didn't even \
know that much about it. "Everything is already done nowadays", "this \
is not where we need to innovate", "don't re-invent the wheel", and \
the best one: "compilers have been made so long ago, they have been \
reviewed by tons of people, they are too big to be reinvented".

p If you were too confident in your work today, this last one should \
have cooled you down. Infact, this is what I heard all the time at \
school when I tried to understand deep things and see how it works.

p What a desilusion when I first tried to play with the "so nice" \
open source tools that are available on the internet. "This is open \
source, you can edit it and make a custom one if you want!" \
Good luck for editing & recompiling your favorite C compiler...

p We are all blind, behind the curtain of our everyday compilers that \
provides us something we don't know everything about. I can't say it \
is bad, but you can't say it is good neither.






t1 ZINC

p This compiler has a big history and has been through a lot of names \
and trials before being what it is now.

t2 First try

p At start, a python version of that Z compiler was written but the \
syntax itself wasn't developed enough for continuing. At this time, \
the compiler was named ZCC for Z Compiler Collection (in reference to \
GCC) because I designed it so that it could be used as a \
multi-language compiler.

p From Z code, unparse a result in a wide \
variety of other languages (lua, kotlin, V...). The command & order \
redirection were in BASH, the compiler in Python and a tiny part was \
in C (for default types information).


t2 The bootstrap query

p When Z came to be more structured on paper, the whole repository \
has been remade. My final goal for ZCC has always been to have a full \
Z written compiler.

p This is a very common thing in low-level languages and compiler, \
and actually, it makes sens: the syntax is made to provide a powerful \
tool for complex development. What better candidate than the compiler \
itself to use it and so, prove its efficiency in multiple algorithmic \
cases.

p On top of that, it allows you to cut any external dependency in the \
compiler so that you can now use your language without having to host \
another environment or anything. Seems really nice but this implies \
that you can compile the code of your own compiler before even having \
it in your hands...

p This "auto-compilation" can be solved using different techniques. \
These techniques allows you to build compilers using other versions \
or fragments of itself: Doing so has a name, we say that we \
"bootstrap" a certain version of our compiler.

p This is definitely not an easy thing to do, especially when you \
don't have an exact & complete idea of your language rules and the \
produced result. The wise advice in that kind of situation would be \
to first have a working compiler using an external resource, and then \
bootstrap it and its further versions.

p I gave some more details about why and how bootstraping a compiler \
in \mdoc/bootstrap.sim\m.




t2 Bootstrap enemy: standards

p For ZINC (ZCC at this moment), I tried several things to make a \
first version completely in C or Python. \
... \
Anyway, I started writting ZCC in full Z.



t2 Two ways development

p Because I did not have a working Z compiler when developing ZCC, I \
had to work blind imaginating the compilation result. Moreover, I \
first thought I could compile manually each file I wrote as soon as I \
finish it but that's not that simple.

p First, to guarantee a real compilation, I must have at least a \
first complete chain from the Z source to the result. I can't test \
anything until I have at least a first version working in C/ASM or \
whatever (even without a number of features). Secondly, compiling \
manually is a \bvery very\b long process so I wanted to do it as less \
as possible.

l Therefore, I decided to change my strategy one more time.

p Now, in the first time, I code every step for both precompilation and \
compilation, \uin Z\u. That represents the huge majority of the time \
spent on ZCC. The main advantage of this period is that I could \
modify the whole Z syntax and ZCC structure at will.

p I did reorganize the ZCC process chain very often, for technical \
reasons. \uI had to write down everything\u to see that a step must \
be executed before another one etc...

p Then, as soon as I had a first complete ZCC working in my head and \
on paper, I could now compile it by hand in C. To do so, I separated \
my screen to have on my left the ZCC steps (in Z) and the C \
translation to the right.

p I DID EXACTLY WHAT ZCC WOULD HAVE DONE IF IT WORKED AT THIS TIME. \
Then, I just had to compile the resulting C with any C compiler and \
so, I would have my first working ZCC executable.

p A way to verify my rigour in hand-compiling is to compile ZCC using \
its freshly created binary. We can now compare the result of a human \
brain with the machine. As soon as I had the very first C version of \
ZCC working, I uploaded it on a dedicated branch in the ZCC repository.

p IT IS CRUCIAL TO KEEP THIS RESULT SOMEWHERE IF I DON'T WANT TO \
COMPILE ZCC MANUALLY ONE MORE TIME. Actually, as soon as I have \
somewhere any version of ZCC executable, there will be no problem \
but I prefer being SAFE.

p By doing such things, we made the first compilation of a Z code (by hand). \
And now, we have the more Z-compilant Z compiler ever. \
By the way, I prefer rely on the machine-compiled version of ZCC than on the manual one.

l Thanks for reading, \arI.A.
